WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.208
(playful music)
(darts hitting)

00:00:01.208 --> 00:00:03.485
- [Narrator] For the Federal
Reserve, controlling inflation

00:00:03.485 --> 00:00:06.420
is a little like playing a game of darts,

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.460
just scored differently.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.170
It uses its tools to aim

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.737
for that bullseye, which in this case is.

00:00:12.737 --> 00:00:13.695
- 2%.

00:00:13.695 --> 00:00:16.530
- 2% inflation target.
- 2% target.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.330
- [Narrator] And just like with darts,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.700
the Fed doesn't always hit the center.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.650
- The process of getting
inflation back down

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.080
to 2% has a long way to go.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.640
- [Narrator] But even getting close

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.310
can help keep inflation in check.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.640
Here's how 2% became the Fed's bullseye

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.920
and why that number can
help guide the health

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.170
of the entire economy.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.880
So where did the Fed come up

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.160
with this target in the first place?

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.800
The answer, New Zealand.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.100
Suffering from persistent
inflation in the 1980s,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.400
the country's reserve bank
initially set an inflation target

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.500
between 0 and 2%.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.270
- Central banks didn't use to tell people

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.340
what they were doing, and in the 1990s,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.050
they began to experiment more with, "Hey,

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.810
if we tell people what it is
we're actually trying to do,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.400
then maybe it'll make it easier

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.500
for us to achieve those goals."

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.780
- [Narrator] It's a
strategy called anchoring.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.970
When consumers and businesses know

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.800
where inflation should run,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.690
it's easier for them to set prices

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.990
and spend in a way that's
consistent with that goal

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.390
and that then helps the Fed set policies

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.670
to maintain that level of inflation.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.140
Think of it as the bullseye

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.690
at the center of the
Fed's monetary policy.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.940
- If you think prices are
gonna be higher in a year,

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.380
you'll demand higher wages now.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.750
So there can be a self-fulfilling element

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.640
to inflation expectations.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.690
- [Narrator] Though targeting has been

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.240
in the Fed's conversation for decades,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.180
the US only officially
adopted inflation targeting

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.840
in 2012 when the Fed was
chaired by Ben Bernanke.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.300
- Clearly communicating
to the public this 2% goal

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.880
for inflation over the longer run

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.590
should help foster price stability

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.810
and moderate long-term interest rates,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.460
and will enhance the committee's ability

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.320
to promote maximum employment

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.530
in the face of significant
economic disturbances.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.830
- [Narrator] So what's so
special about the number two?

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.080
- There isn't a lot of empirical research

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.780
that says we've analyzed
all the different outcomes

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.030
and 2% is the right
number for these reasons.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.260
That's really not how central
bankers came up with 2%.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.740
The idea was you wanted to
have a a level of inflation

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.530
that was low enough that
people wouldn't pay attention

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.690
to how high prices were going up.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.830
- [Narrator] For the Fed,

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.530
the number two achieves a few things.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.690
For starters, it's not so low

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.650
that the economy risks deflation,
which is when prices fall

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.340
and can lead to lower wages
and higher unemployment.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.380
The economy was at risk for deflation

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.340
during the Great Recession
between 2007 and 2009.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.680
- Falling prices for a
central bank can actually

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.860
be a worse state of
affairs than rising prices

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.500
because if people think prices
are gonna be lower tomorrow,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.700
they won't spend today

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.400
and that can be very hard to get out of.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.520
- [Narrator] Lower inflation
also reduces the Fed's ability

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.350
to lower interest rates

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.440
because rates tend to move
together with inflation.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.300
- If you have a 0% target,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.280
you'll probably have lower interest rates

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.710
and the concern would
be you'd have less room

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.040
to stimulate the economy
when you go into a recession.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.820
- [Narrator] But 2% is also
thought of as not too high.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.910
High inflation can weaken
consumers' spending power.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:18.900
- The concern would be

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.570
that inflation might
actually creep too high

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:22.920
and then you'd get into a world

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.410
of self-sustaining price increases

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.200
where higher prices feed on themselves

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.230
and that's a cycle the Fed
doesn't wanna find itself in.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.510
- [Narrator] The Fed wants
inflation to hit as close

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.770
to 2% as it can over time to
maintain a healthy economy.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.200
But sometimes it misses.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.230
Through the 2010s, some
economists were concerned

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.770
that inflation was
consistently running too low.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.110
A new policy in 2020 called

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.810
flexible average inflation targeting tries

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.240
to account for some of those periods.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.030
Rather than always
aiming for the bullseye,

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.790
the Fed tries to set policy
to balance out periods

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.660
of lower inflation with periods
of slightly higher inflation

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.990
with the goal of averaging
out at 2% over time.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.490
- The worry was that the anchor

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:12.270
on inflation expectations was
actually drifting too low.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.150
And so you could recenter
it by saying, look,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.910
we really wanna make sure
people don't think 2%

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.170
is a hard ceiling.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:20.003
It's a target.

00:04:20.003 --> 00:04:21.360
If we're below it for a little while,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.320
we can be above it somewhat
for a little while.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.880
- [Narrator] But not everyone agrees

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.310
with the Fed's 2% target strategy.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.390
One concern is that monetary
policy is imprecise.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.520
- You know, monetary
policy's a blunt instrument.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.353
This is more

00:04:36.353 --> 00:04:39.540
like going to see a barber
than having a surgery.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.240
- [Narrator] Some also argue
that a higher rate of inflation

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.940
leaves more room for the
Fed to adjust interest rates

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.263
to avoid a recession.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.408
Whether or not inflation
targeting is actually effective

00:04:50.408 --> 00:04:52.470
is difficult to measure.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.500
Inflation did settle for
a time after the policy

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.260
was implemented in New Zealand in the '90s

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.080
in conjunction with a
number of other measures,

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.050
and it's still relatively new in the US.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.010
- As we've discovered,

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.560
there are shocks that can hit the economy

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.580
and it's difficult to
judge in real time I think

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.830
whether this is the right strategy.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.780
- Currently, the Fed reviews the framework

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.660
of its monetary policy every five years.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.480
- The idea that the Fed would
make some kind of change

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.600
to its inflation target in
the next couple of years seems

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.780
like a big stretch, but they
could debate at the margins,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.370
are there different nuances

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:32.520
that our formal strategy
document hasn't captured?

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.050
And those are the sorts of things

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.840
that you might expect to
see in the deliberations

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.270
around any further
review of the framework.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.103
- [Narrator] But for now.

00:05:40.103 --> 00:05:40.936
- 2%.

00:05:40.936 --> 00:05:43.707
- [Narrator] Remains the
bullseye it will keep aiming for.

00:05:43.707 --> 00:05:46.374
(playful music)

